 "literal": "Living with the days, years many, because of existence / leaving not going, my life, saying, lamenting much / staff as leg, short many steps walking slowly / thread spread-out like hair-having-she, eyes having slept / courtyard not going, advanced age-having-she, my mother and / paled body-with, grief afflicting, suffering / on her waist held, many small children / squeezing to feed, withered breast-having-she, greatly despairing / rubbish heap greens plucked-place sprouted / unmatured tender shoots having plucked, without salt / water as boiling pot placing, without buttermilk / cooked rice state forgetting, green leaves eating / with dirt worn out garment-having-she, virtue not blaming / not enjoying who has become, my beloved wife and / thus both their hearts to rejoice, forest dwellers / burnt forest field cleared wide-spaced dry land / mountain rice sowing, to become dark and beautiful / yielding not yet proceeding millet, with a rushing sound / clouds rain having poured down everywhere / given your fame praising, gathered my / hunger consuming withered kinfolk to rejoice / rising high held up tusk-with killer male elephant even if obtaining / hesitatingly given gift I will not accept; rejoicing you / pleasurably give if, a small / crab's eye seed even I will accept, O sharp-speared Kumanan! / In the proper way to bestow I request, O victorious fame / faultless noble lineage born / O lord eminent in fame, you who sang, I indeed!",
 "translation": "My life does not leave me, though I lament, 'Why do I go on living for so many years?' My aged mother, who uses a staff as a leg, takes short, slow steps, has hair like unraveled thread, and never leaves the courtyard. My beloved wife, her body paled with grief, holds many small children on her waist who squeeze her withered breasts to feed; in deep despair, she plucks the young, unsalted shoots of greens sprouted from the rubbish heap, boils them in water without buttermilk, and eats them, forgetting the taste of cooked rice, wearing a dirty, tattered garment yet never blaming virtue. To make both their hearts rejoice, just as the dark clouds pour down rain with a rushing sound over the wide dry lands cleared by forest dwellers where mountain rice is sown but the millet has not yet yielded, I have come praising your fame, so that my kinfolk, withered by hunger, may also rejoice. Even if I were to receive a killer elephant with high, upraised tusks, I would not accept a gift given with hesitation. But if you give with a joyful heart, O Kumanan of the sharp spear, I will gladly accept even a tiny seed! I beg you to bestow your grace in the proper way, O lord of victorious fame, born of a faultless noble lineage, eminent in renown! I am the poet who has sung of you!",
 "modern": "வாழும் நாட்கள் பலவாக இருப்பதால் என் உயிர் என்னை விட்டுப் பிரியாமல் இருக்கிறது என்று வருந்தி, கோலையே காலாகக் கொண்டு மெல்ல நடந்து, முன்றிலைத் தாண்டாத என் வயதான தாயும்; வறுமையால் மெலிந்து, தன் இடுப்பில் பல குழந்தைகளைச் சுமந்து, பசியால் வாடிய முலைகளைக் கொண்ட என் மனைவியும்; குப்பையில் முளைத்த கீரையின் தளிர் இலைகளைப் பறித்து, உப்பும் மோரும் இன்றி வெற்று நீரில் வேகவைத்து, சோற்றின் சுவையை மறந்து உண்ணும் என் அன்பான மனைவியும்; ஆகிய இவ்விருவரின் நெஞ்சமும் மகிழும்படி, காட்டில் தினை விதைத்து மழைக்காகக் காத்திருக்கும் நிலத்தில் மேகம் பொழிவது போல, உனது புகழைப் பாடி வந்த என் சுற்றத்தாரின் பசியும் நீங்கி மகிழும்படி; உயர்ந்த தந்தங்களைக் கொண்ட யானையைப் பரிசாகத் தந்தாலும், அதனை மனமில்லாமல் தந்தால் நான் ஏற்றுக்கொள்ள மாட்டேன். ஆனால், கூர்மையான வேலைக் கொண்ட குமணனே! நீ மகிழ்ந்து அன்போடு கொடுத்தால், ஒரு சிறிய குன்றிமணியின் அளவேயான பரிசையும் நான் ஏற்பேன். புகழும் நற்குடியும் கொண்ட பெருமகனே! உன்னைப் பாடி வந்த எனக்கு முறைப்படி அருள் செய்ய வேண்டுகிறேன்.",
 "explanation": "This poem is set in the Puram genre, specifically under the theme of requesting a gift (parisil kadānilai) from King Kumanan. The poet, Perunchithiranar, vividly describes the extreme poverty of his household to evoke the patron's generosity. He details the suffering of his aged mother, who can barely walk, and his devoted wife, who must feed their children with unsalted wild greens boiled in water, having forgotten the taste of cooked rice. The poet compares his arrival at the king's court to the arrival of rain over a parched field, hoping to relieve his starving kinfolk. Crucially, Perunchithiranar asserts his dignity as a poet: he declares that he would reject even a magnificent elephant if it were given with hesitation or without respect, but would gladly accept a tiny seed if offered with a joyful, loving heart. Key words include 'pasadagu' (green leaves), 'parisil' (gift), and 'kumanan' (the patron king).",
